=====================
The "unfixed" AI type
=====================

What it is
----------

A frozen copy of the stock AI, bugs and all, built as a separate AI
module so that a server whose ai/default has been fixed can play the
fixed AI against the unfixed one in the same game:

  ai/default/*.[ch]             -> ai/unfixed/default/   (verbatim)
  ai/classic/classicai.[ch]     -> ai/unfixed/classic/   (verbatim)

copied from upstream main at

  9d0461dad023f6d22768275a0323a0ae20cf33d7
  ("cityresult_fill(): Clean up on every early return", 2026-11-02)

Build it with -Daimodules=classic,tex,unfixed (meson only; it is off by
default and autotools does not know it). It is then selectable like any
AI type:

  create Foo unfixed      one player
  set aitype unfixed      aifill, barbarian and animal players

How it links beside ai/default
------------------------------

The copied files are byte-identical to their originals. The build
force-includes unfixed_syms.h (-include, see fc_ai_unfixed in
meson.build) into each of them; it #defines every global symbol that
ai/default and ai/classic define to an unf_ name. It is generated by
scripts/unfixed-syms.sh from nm over the compiled originals.

The only new code is unfixedai.c. It runs the vendored classic setup,
names the type "unfixed", and replaces the six savegame hooks with ones
that save under the prefixes "unfixed" and "unfixed_": every
registered module's save hook runs for every player, and the vendored
wrappers' own prefixes ("ai", and "" for units) are already the live
classic module's.

It must never be edited
-----------------------

Its value is that it is provably the stock AI. Do not fix bugs here,
however obvious; fix them in ai/default, where the unfixed copy can
measure the fix.

scripts/check-unfixed-frozen.sh, run as the meson test
"unfixed-frozen" whenever the module is built, enforces this. It checks
every file against MANIFEST.sha256, checks that the manifest's hash for
each copied file is its original's at the commit above, and checks
that the built module defines every renamed symbol and references no
symbol of the live ai/default or ai/classic. A failure means the copy
changed; revert the change, never regenerate the manifest. Re-vendoring
from a newer ai/default is a new copy, a regenerated unfixed_syms.h and
manifest, a new commit hash here, and a new identity run, not an edit.

This README is the one file the manifest leaves out, so it can be
corrected without touching the frozen code.

That the module plays exactly like the stock AI is shown by
scripts/unfixed-identity.py: the same all-AI game, played once with
every player "classic" and once with every player "unfixed", gives
identical savegames on every turn, up to the fields that name the
module and three wall-clock fields (the script lists them). At
vendoring this held for seeds 1-3 on both classic and civ2civ3, 60
turns each. Rerun it after any change to the build or to the shared
code it depends on:

  scripts/unfixed-identity.py --server build/freeciv-server \
    --data data --workdir $TMPDIR/identity --ruleset classic \
    --seeds 1,2,3 --turns 60

Its known limit
---------------

Only ai/default and ai/classic are copied. server/advisors/, common/,
common/aicore/ and the rest of the server are shared by both modules,
so a fix there changes the unfixed AI as well. A head-to-head between
"classic" and "unfixed" measures only differences inside ai/default and
ai/classic.
